WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.500
A smart pointer safety roadmap starts with ownership design.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:13.720
Before choosing a type, decide whether the object should be a value, uniquely owned, shared, weakly observed, or borrowed for a short operation.

3
00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:20.620
Prefer value ownership when it is practical because it keeps lifetime tied directly to scope and containing objects.

4
00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:25.680
When dynamic exclusive ownership is needed, prefer std::unique_ptr .

5
00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:29.240
Use std::shared_ptr deliberately.

6
00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:34.140
It is a tool for real shared lifetime, not a substitute for deciding ownership.

7
00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:42.880
Review shared ownership graphs for cycles, accidental lifetime extension, retained sensitive resources, and unclear shutdown behavior.

8
00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:51.380
Use std::weak_ptr when code needs to observe a shared object without keeping it alive, and handle expiration as part of the normal design.

9
00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:53.180
Make APIs say what they mean.

10
00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.440
Transfer ownership with types that show transfer.

11
00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:04.020
Borrow with references or raw pointers when the access is short-lived and the lifetime contract is clear.

12
00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:12.440
Use RAII and correct deleters for resources beyond memory, and wrap recurring cleanup rules in simple resource-owning types.

13
00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:17.440
Finally, treat smart pointer use as part of normal secure engineering.

14
00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:23.860
Test destruction order, error paths, callbacks, async work, and legacy migration boundaries.

15
00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:27.720
Use diagnostics and code review to follow ownership graphs.

16
00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:36.620
The goal is practical: fewer hidden lifetimes, fewer manual cleanup mistakes, and C++ code that remains understandable under maintenance and change.
